3587
01:50:08,665 --> 01:50:10,833
<b>is essentially the architecture of a neural network.</b>

3588
01:50:10,833 --> 01:50:11,634
<b>Mm.</b>

3589
01:50:11,634 --> 01:50:13,870
<b>But having just the engine's architecture</b>

3590
01:50:13,870 --> 01:50:14,871
<b>is actually useless.</b>

3591
01:50:15,138 --> 01:50:16,005
<b>You have no fuel.</b>

3592
01:50:16,005 --> 01:50:17,073
<b>You can't start it.</b>

3593
01:50:17,340 --> 01:50:19,242
<b>Right. So, uh,</b>

3594
01:50:19,242 --> 01:50:20,443
<b>there's the data dimension</b>

3595
01:50:20,443 --> 01:50:22,045
<b>and there's the objective dimension,</b>

3596
01:50:22,045 --> 01:50:24,647
<b>the objective function considerations.</b>

3597
01:50:25,415 --> 01:50:27,383
<b>And, so</b>

3598
01:50:27,383 --> 01:50:28,618
<b>My subsequent research</b>

3599
01:50:28,618 --> 01:50:31,020
<b>has also followed this main thread —</b>

3600
01:50:31,020 --> 01:50:32,555
<b>representation learning as the main thread —</b>

3601
01:50:32,622 --> 01:50:37,527
<b>advancing around architecture, data, and objective.</b>

3602
01:50:37,527 --> 01:50:38,628
<b>Mm-hmm.</b>

3603
01:50:39,128 --> 01:50:40,563
<b>And, uh,</b>

3604
01:50:40,563 --> 01:50:42,966
<b>During the time at FAIR,</b>

3605
01:50:42,966 --> 01:50:43,766
<b>I think FAIR</b>

3606
01:50:44,367 --> 01:50:45,268
<b>— this full-time job,</b>

3607
01:50:45,268 --> 01:50:46,736
<b>in the full-time work process —</b>

3608
01:50:46,736 --> 01:50:49,872
<b>I think one core aspect was</b>

3609
01:50:49,872 --> 01:50:51,641
<b>that I worked with Kaiming,</b>

3610
01:50:51,641 --> 01:50:54,043
<b>and Kaiming was leading some</b>

3611
01:50:54,043 --> 01:50:55,211
<b>self-supervised learning</b>

3612
01:50:55,211 --> 01:50:56,012
<b>such work,</b>

3613
01:50:56,379 --> 01:50:56,980
<b>Right.</b>

3614
01:50:56,980 --> 01:50:58,514
<b>And actually, again,</b>

3615
01:50:58,514 --> 01:51:01,517
<b>now everyone says Scaling is</b>

3616
01:51:01,517 --> 01:51:01,985
<b>is</b>

3617
01:51:01,985 --> 01:51:02,986
<b>already a buzzword.</b>

3618
01:51:02,986 --> 01:51:04,721
<b>Everybody's talking about scaling.</b>

3619
01:51:04,721 --> 01:51:05,555
<b>Mm. Right.</b>

3620
01:51:05,588 --> 01:51:09,058
<b>But actually the first person who really told me</b>

3621
01:51:09,158 --> 01:51:11,127
<b>that we need a scalable model,</b>

3622
01:51:11,127 --> 01:51:14,397
<b>that we need to make the model bigger and bigger,</b>

3623
01:51:14,397 --> 01:51:15,632
<b>these were Kaiming's exact words.</b>

3624
01:51:15,665 --> 01:51:16,265
<b>Bigger and bigger.</b>

3625
01:51:16,265 --> 01:51:18,067
<b>Right, yes.</b>

3626
01:51:18,067 --> 01:51:19,202
<b>Kaiming told me this.</b>

3627
01:51:19,369 --> 01:51:20,803
<b>What year did he tell you?</b>

3628
01:51:20,803 --> 01:51:25,008
<b>Uh, roughly around 2018 or 2019.</b>

3629
01:51:25,008 --> 01:51:25,742
<b>Right. And then</b>

3630
01:51:25,742 --> 01:51:28,144
<b>So from the very beginning his conviction was</b>

3631
01:51:28,144 --> 01:51:28,478
<b>that is,</b>

3632
01:51:28,478 --> 01:51:32,615
<b>we must make models bigger,</b>

3633
01:51:32,615 --> 01:51:34,450
<b>make data bigger,</b>

3634
01:51:34,484 --> 01:51:36,619
<b>and this would allow us to get</b>

3635
01:51:36,619 --> 01:51:38,221
<b>a better result.</b>

3636
01:51:38,388 --> 01:51:40,023
<b>I think very early on,</b>

3637
01:51:40,023 --> 01:51:41,124
<b>Kaiming already had this vision.</b>

3638
01:51:41,224 --> 01:51:42,025
<b>Mm.</b>

3639
01:51:42,592 --> 01:51:43,593
<b>Uh. And then</b>

3640
01:51:43,593 --> 01:51:44,961
<b>so we also</b>

3641
01:51:44,961 --> 01:51:47,964
<b>made some efforts along this path.</b>

3642
01:51:47,964 --> 01:51:49,766
<b>And so I think</b>

3643
01:51:49,766 --> 01:51:52,068
<b>initially, the discussion about self-supervised learning —</b>

3644
01:51:52,068 --> 01:51:53,569
<b>including Yann,</b>

3645
01:51:53,870 --> 01:51:54,203
<b>Uh,</b>

3646
01:51:54,203 --> 01:51:56,272
<b>he's a big advocate.</b>

3647
01:51:56,272 --> 01:51:57,206
<b>That is, he is</b>

3648
01:51:57,273 --> 01:51:58,608
<b>very invested in</b>

3649
01:51:58,608 --> 01:51:59,008
<b>self-supervised learning —</b>

3650
01:51:59,008 --> 01:52:01,611
<b>He has this classic cake analogy.</b>

3651
01:52:01,611 --> 01:52:02,578
<b>This metaphor.</b>

3652
01:52:02,578 --> 01:52:04,947
<b>Right, the base layer is</b>

3653
01:52:05,114 --> 01:52:07,216
<b>the body of the cake,</b>

3654
01:52:07,250 --> 01:52:09,919
<b>and this part must be Self-Supervised Learning.</b>

3655
01:52:10,219 --> 01:52:12,588
<b>On top of that you can have Supervised Learning,</b>

3656
01:52:12,722 --> 01:52:14,991
<b>right, this is the icing on the cake,</b>

3657
01:52:15,024 --> 01:52:18,528
<b>the cream on your cake.</b>

3658
01:52:18,528 --> 01:52:21,564
<b>And further on top is Reinforcement Learning,</b>

3659
01:52:21,564 --> 01:52:22,865
<b>it's just the cherry on top,</b>

3660
01:52:22,865 --> 01:52:24,867
<b>just a little cherry at the very top.</b>

3661
01:52:24,867 --> 01:52:25,768
<b>Mm.</b>

3662
01:52:26,169 --> 01:52:29,639
<b>Each layer of this cake is actually important,</b>

3663
01:52:29,772 --> 01:52:31,441
<b>but they're not ranked by importance.</b>

3664
01:52:32,041 --> 01:52:32,642
<b>Mm.</b>

3665
01:52:32,642 --> 01:52:35,344
<b>If you don't have the cake's base,</b>

3666
01:52:35,344 --> 01:52:37,914
<b>you can't get to intelligence</b>

3667
01:52:38,014 --> 01:52:39,415
<b>relying only on the cherry on top.</b>

3668
01:52:39,415 --> 01:52:40,216
<b>Mm.</b>

3669
01:52:40,516 --> 01:52:42,552
<b>Right. So because we were at FAIR</b>

3670
01:52:42,552 --> 01:52:43,786
<b>doing vision,</b>

3671
01:52:44,187 --> 01:52:46,589
<b>we were actually paying attention to this very early.</b>

3672
01:52:47,156 --> 01:52:49,425
<b>But the process of this research went like this:</b>

3673
01:52:49,425 --> 01:52:52,228
<b>around 2015 and 2016,</b>

3674
01:52:52,528 --> 01:52:55,998
<b>people already knew that self-supervised learning</b>

3675
01:52:55,998 --> 01:52:57,967
<b>was actually a future for vision.</b>

3676
01:52:58,634 --> 01:53:00,336
<b>So at that time, uh,</b>

3677
01:53:00,336 --> 01:53:01,237
<b>people would design</b>

3678
01:53:01,237 --> 01:53:02,672
<b>all kinds of</b>

3679
01:53:02,672 --> 01:53:05,074
<b>what we call pretext tasks,</b>

3680
01:53:05,074 --> 01:53:07,043
<b>or proxy</b>

3681
01:53:07,043 --> 01:53:09,345
<b>objective goals,</b>

3682
01:53:09,345 --> 01:53:10,646
<b>some proxy tasks.</b>

3683
01:53:10,813 --> 01:53:12,014
<b>that is,</b>

3684
01:53:12,014 --> 01:53:13,116
<b>what is self-supervised learning?</b>

3685
01:53:13,116 --> 01:53:16,119
<b>I don't have a label to directly give you,</b>

3686
01:53:16,352 --> 01:53:18,421
<b>unlike ImageNet,</b>

3687
01:53:18,421 --> 01:53:20,123
<b>where I have 1000 classes</b>

3688
01:53:20,123 --> 01:53:21,424
<b>and can directly train</b>

3689
01:53:21,424 --> 01:53:23,192
<b>a supervised classifier</b>

3690
01:53:23,326 --> 01:53:26,662
<b>and get a representation this way.</b>

3691
01:53:26,996 --> 01:53:28,064
<b>In the old days,</b>

3692
01:53:28,064 --> 01:53:29,532
<b>this is what everyone was doing.</b>

3693
01:53:29,532 --> 01:53:32,368
<b>Through 1000 class labels, by the way,</b>

3694
01:53:32,368 --> 01:53:35,138
<b>within these 1000 classes there are 200 dog</b>

3695
01:53:35,138 --> 01:53:36,572
<b>different breeds.</b>

3696
01:53:36,572 --> 01:53:37,673
<b>Even so,</b>

3697
01:53:37,673 --> 01:53:39,242
<b>this is why</b>

3698
01:53:39,242 --> 01:53:40,576
<b>ImageNet is so powerful.</b>

3699
01:53:40,576 --> 01:53:42,578
<b>Right? Even with that distribution,</b>

3700
01:53:42,578 --> 01:53:44,380
<b>it can still let</b>

3701
01:53:44,380 --> 01:53:46,215
<b>our neural networks learn good representations.</b>

3702
01:53:46,249 --> 01:53:47,650
<b>I think this is extremely impressive.</b>

3703
01:53:47,650 --> 01:53:49,786
<b>But people also see the limitations.</b>

3704
01:53:50,153 --> 01:53:52,622
<b>Once everything is just Supervised Learning,</b>

3705
01:53:52,855 --> 01:53:55,792
<b>there are many things you can't capture.</b>

3706
01:53:55,825 --> 01:53:56,826
<b>Mm.</b>

3707
01:53:56,826 --> 01:53:58,161
<b>Because what it learns</b>

3708
01:53:58,161 --> 01:53:59,462
<b>— for example, we're sitting here now,</b>

3709
01:53:59,462 --> 01:54:00,797
<b>we see these chairs,</b>

3710
01:54:00,830 --> 01:54:01,564
<b>Right?</b>

3711
01:54:01,564 --> 01:54:04,133
<b>and we now have a lot of images,</b>

3712
01:54:04,133 --> 01:54:05,802
<b>of different chairs.</b>

3713
01:54:05,802 --> 01:54:07,436
<b>Some chairs might be quite ordinary,</b>

3714
01:54:07,436 --> 01:54:08,971
<b>chairs in a studio like ours,</b>

3715
01:54:08,971 --> 01:54:10,373
<b>or chairs in a home,</b>

3716
01:54:10,473 --> 01:54:13,009
<b>or some designer chairs,</b>

3717
01:54:13,009 --> 01:54:15,344
<b>right, or like an avocado chair,</b>

3718
01:54:15,344 --> 01:54:16,813
<b>a chair shaped like an avocado.</b>

3719
01:54:17,380 --> 01:54:19,549
<b>For supervised learning,</b>

3720
01:54:19,549 --> 01:54:21,451
<b>you need to map all of this</b>

3721
01:54:21,617 --> 01:54:23,319
<b>to a single label,</b>

3722
01:54:23,319 --> 01:54:26,155
<b>this label is called "chair".</b>

3723
01:54:26,189 --> 01:54:28,925
<b>So what your network has to learn,</b>

3724
01:54:28,925 --> 01:54:30,326
<b>this mapping,</b>

3725
01:54:30,326 --> 01:54:31,961
<b>is actually very, very difficult.</b>

3726
01:54:32,328 --> 01:54:33,129
<b>Right.</b>

3727
01:54:33,229 --> 01:54:34,831
<b>And it's an infinite mapping.</b>

3728
01:54:34,831 --> 01:54:35,932
<b>It's an infinite mapping.</b>

3729
01:54:35,932 --> 01:54:36,766
<b>Mm.</b>

3730
01:54:36,799 --> 01:54:41,270
<b>So it can only either memorize,</b>

3731
01:54:41,270 --> 01:54:42,605
<b>just remember,</b>

3732
01:54:42,605 --> 01:54:44,373
<b>recite all the chairs it's ever seen,</b>

3733
01:54:46,209 --> 01:54:48,344
<b>or</b>

3734
01:54:48,444 --> 01:54:49,812
<b>this,</b>

3735
01:54:49,812 --> 01:54:52,548
<b>through what we call spurious correlations,</b>

3736
01:54:52,548 --> 01:54:55,318
<b>some false correlations,</b>

3737
01:54:55,418 --> 01:54:56,752
<b>tell you it's a chair.</b>

3738
01:54:56,752 --> 01:54:58,321
<b>For example, it may not look at the chair itself</b>

3739
01:54:58,321 --> 01:55:00,756
<b>but look at the background behind the chair,</b>

3740
01:55:00,756 --> 01:55:02,225
<b>or it thinks</b>

3741
01:55:02,225 --> 01:55:04,460
<b>all chairs will be next to a table,</b>

3742
01:55:04,460 --> 01:55:07,129
<b>so it uses that to make a decision boundary</b>

3743
01:55:07,129 --> 01:55:07,730
<b>and says,</b>

3744
01:55:07,730 --> 01:55:08,598
<b>hey, this is a chair.</b>

3745
01:55:08,731 --> 01:55:10,199
<b>But this is not what we want.</b>

3746
01:55:10,366 --> 01:55:11,534
<b>What we want to achieve</b>

3747
01:55:11,534 --> 01:55:15,004
<b>is, from this very diverse visual knowledge,</b>

3748
01:55:15,004 --> 01:55:18,274
<b>these visual observations, to gain some kind of common sense,</b>

3749
01:55:18,274 --> 01:55:22,445
<b>some kind of intuition.</b>

3750
01:55:22,545 --> 01:55:23,779
<b>Mm. Intuition.</b>

3751
01:55:24,046 --> 01:55:26,582
<b>Right. Or some kind of common understanding.</b>

3752
01:55:26,716 --> 01:55:29,819
<b>So this is why people initially wanted to do</b>

3753
01:55:29,819 --> 01:55:30,953
<b>so-called Self-Supervised Learning</b>

3754
01:55:30,953 --> 01:55:31,854
<b>or Unsupervised Learning.</b>

3755
01:55:31,854 --> 01:55:34,824
<b>A common misconception back then was</b>

3756
01:55:34,824 --> 01:55:35,658
<b>people say</b>

3757
01:55:35,658 --> 01:55:36,993
<b>we want to do Unsupervised Learning</b>

3758
01:55:36,993 --> 01:55:40,129
<b>because labeling data</b>

3759
01:55:40,229 --> 01:55:41,430
<b>is too hard and too expensive.</b>

3760
01:55:41,430 --> 01:55:42,598
<b>We need to hire people</b>

3761
01:55:42,832 --> 01:55:45,001
<b>to label,</b>

3762
01:55:45,001 --> 01:55:46,002
<b>spending money and time.</b>

3763
01:55:46,002 --> 01:55:47,003
<b>We don't want to do that.</b>

3764
01:55:47,003 --> 01:55:47,436
<b>But that's just</b>

3765
01:55:47,436 --> 01:55:49,772
<b>one very small part of the problem.</b>

3766
01:55:49,939 --> 01:55:53,743
<b>The bigger issue is, in the eyes of computer vision researchers,</b>

3767
01:55:53,776 --> 01:55:54,143
<b>ah,</b>

3768
01:55:54,143 --> 01:55:56,245
<b>everyone knew long ago</b>

3769
01:55:56,245 --> 01:55:57,580
<b>that only through this path</b>

3770
01:55:57,613 --> 01:56:01,150
<b>there's no way to give AI systems this kind of common sense.</b>

3771
01:56:01,150 --> 01:56:03,519
<b>So in 2015 and 2016,</b>

3772
01:56:03,519 --> 01:56:04,754
<b>everyone was very, very creative.</b>

3773
01:56:04,754 --> 01:56:05,354
<b>That period</b>

3774
01:56:05,354 --> 01:56:07,323
<b>was actually a quite creative era.</b>

3775
01:56:07,456 --> 01:56:09,158
<b>People would design</b>

3776
01:56:09,158 --> 01:56:10,860
<b>all kinds of crazy tasks.</b>

3777
01:56:10,860 --> 01:56:11,327
<b>These tasks —</b>

3778
01:56:11,327 --> 01:56:13,863
<b>for example, you take an image,</b>

3779
01:56:13,863 --> 01:56:14,797
<b>rotate it 90 degrees,</b>

3780
01:56:14,797 --> 01:56:15,831
<b>or 180 degrees,</b>

3781
01:56:15,831 --> 01:56:17,166
<b>or 270 degrees.</b>

3782
01:56:17,400 --> 01:56:19,135
<b>You don't give these images a label,</b>

3783
01:56:19,201 --> 01:56:20,369
<b>but because you designed</b>

3784
01:56:20,536 --> 01:56:22,605
<b>how to rotate these images,</b>

3785
01:56:22,672 --> 01:56:25,641
<b>right, and these images</b>

3786
01:56:25,641 --> 01:56:27,043
<b>and their</b>

3787
01:56:27,043 --> 01:56:28,744
<b>rotation angles</b>

3788
01:56:28,878 --> 01:56:31,781
<b>can form a valid pretext task.</b>

3789
01:56:31,814 --> 01:56:34,717
<b>You can predict how these rotated images</b>

3790
01:56:34,784 --> 01:56:36,118
<b>were actually rotated.</b>

3791
01:56:36,519 --> 01:56:38,054
<b>This becomes a so-called</b>

3792
01:56:38,054 --> 01:56:39,121
<b>proxy task.</b>

3793
01:56:39,121 --> 01:56:39,922
<b>Mm.</b>

3794
01:56:39,956 --> 01:56:42,024
<b>Similar proxy tasks also include</b>

3795
01:56:42,024 --> 01:56:43,426
<b>giving an image,</b>

3796
01:56:43,426 --> 01:56:45,528
<b>converting it to grayscale,</b>

3797
01:56:45,528 --> 01:56:46,929
<b>removing all its colors,</b>

3798
01:56:47,263 --> 01:56:49,565
<b>but then using a neural network</b>

3799
01:56:49,632 --> 01:56:51,400
<b>to reconstruct the original colors.</b>

3800
01:56:51,400 --> 01:56:52,802
<b>Essentially, from a grayscale image,</b>

3801
01:56:52,835 --> 01:56:54,370
<b>how do you predict</b>

3802
01:56:54,370 --> 01:56:56,238
<b>the color of each object</b>

3803
01:56:56,238 --> 01:56:57,740
<b>as it should be.</b>

3804
01:56:58,040 --> 01:56:58,841
<b>Mm.</b>

3805
01:56:58,841 --> 01:57:01,143
<b>And there are other similar examples,</b>

3806
01:57:01,143 --> 01:57:02,211
<b>too many to count.</b>

3807
01:57:02,211 --> 01:57:04,914
<b>Another example, one last one:</b>

3808
01:57:04,914 --> 01:57:05,748
<b>let me give one more example.</b>

3809
01:57:05,748 --> 01:57:07,149
<b>The so-called Context Encoder —</b>

3810
01:57:07,149 --> 01:57:09,552
<b>you take an image, cut out a piece in the middle,</b>

3811
01:57:09,552 --> 01:57:10,419
<b>make it white,</b>

3812
01:57:10,753 --> 01:57:13,222
<b>and then train a neural network</b>

3813
01:57:13,356 --> 01:57:15,358
<b>to fill in this empty part.</b>

3814
01:57:15,358 --> 01:57:16,459
<b>Fill it in.</b>

3815
01:57:16,459 --> 01:57:16,826
<b>Mm.</b>

3816
01:57:16,826 --> 01:57:20,830
<b>The rationale behind all these pretext tasks is</b>

3817
01:57:20,830 --> 01:57:22,264
<b>that</b>

3818
01:57:22,264 --> 01:57:24,367
<b>humans can actually do this.</b>

3819
01:57:24,667 --> 01:57:25,935
<b>The reason humans can do this,</b>

3820
01:57:25,935 --> 01:57:27,169
<b>the reason humans know,</b>

3821
01:57:27,169 --> 01:57:27,570
<b>hey,</b>

3822
01:57:27,570 --> 01:57:30,239
<b>whether this image was rotated 90 or 180 degrees,</b>

3823
01:57:30,339 --> 01:57:33,676
<b>or what color the butterfly</b>

3824
01:57:33,676 --> 01:57:34,443
<b>or house in this image should be,</b>

3825
01:57:34,443 --> 01:57:35,845
<b>what color should it have,</b>

3826
01:57:36,045 --> 01:57:36,379
<b>or</b>

3827
01:57:36,379 --> 01:57:39,515
<b>you can predict the information missing in the middle —</b>

3828
01:57:39,915 --> 01:57:41,050
<b>all these things</b>

3829
01:57:41,050 --> 01:57:41,784
<b>is because humans,</b>

3830
01:57:42,018 --> 01:57:45,221
<b>based on some understanding of the physical world,</b>

3831
01:57:45,221 --> 01:57:46,322
<b>have this common sense,</b>

3832
01:57:46,322 --> 01:57:48,958
<b>so they can guess</b>

3833
01:57:48,958 --> 01:57:50,559
<b>these corrupted signals,</b>

3834
01:57:50,559 --> 01:57:52,828
<b>these already lost signals,</b>

3835
01:57:52,828 --> 01:57:54,764
<b>how they should be reconstructed.</b>

3836
01:57:54,764 --> 01:57:55,798
<b>The masked signals.</b>

3837
01:57:55,798 --> 01:57:56,432
<b>Right.</b>

3838
01:57:56,432 --> 01:57:58,768
<b>But back then the problem was a hundred flowers blooming —</b>

3839
01:57:58,768 --> 01:57:59,869
<b>all kinds of papers,</b>

3840
01:58:00,236 --> 01:58:01,037
<b>Mm.</b>

3841
01:58:01,137 --> 01:58:03,939
<b>but none of them worked well.</b>

3842
01:58:03,939 --> 01:58:06,075
<b>All the results were actually quite poor,</b>

3843
01:58:06,075 --> 01:58:09,578
<b>all worse than ImageNet pre-training,</b>

3844
01:58:09,578 --> 01:58:11,781
<b>by roughly 15-20 percentage points.</b>

3845
01:58:11,781 --> 01:58:13,349
<b>Percentage points.</b>

3846
01:58:13,349 --> 01:58:15,584
<b>So people were making some progress,</b>

3847
01:58:15,584 --> 01:58:17,153
<b>moving forward step by step,</b>

3848
01:58:17,420 --> 01:58:19,588
<b>but the gap</b>

3849
01:58:19,789 --> 01:58:22,925
<b>uh, what ImageNet could achieve through Supervised Learning,</b>

3850
01:58:22,925 --> 01:58:25,227
<b>learned on large-scale data,</b>

3851
01:58:25,227 --> 01:58:26,429
<b>with labels,</b>

3852
01:58:26,595 --> 01:58:26,996
<b>Uh,</b>

3853
01:58:26,996 --> 01:58:29,632
<b>the representation learned with labels,</b>

3854
01:58:29,732 --> 01:58:30,966
<b>was still far, far better.</b>

3855
01:58:31,267 --> 01:58:31,834
<b>Right?</b>

3856
01:58:31,834 --> 01:58:33,235
<b>So, uh,</b>

3857
01:58:33,235 --> 01:58:35,471
<b>we did something at that time,</b>

3858
01:58:35,471 --> 01:58:36,739
<b>and this was done together with Kaiming.</b>

3859
01:58:36,739 --> 01:58:37,173
<b>And this,</b>

3860
01:58:37,173 --> 01:58:38,340
<b>this architecture is called</b>

3861
01:58:38,340 --> 01:58:39,275
<b>called MoCo,</b>

3862
01:58:39,341 --> 01:58:39,742
<b>Mm.</b>

3863
01:58:39,742 --> 01:58:41,210
<b>Momentum Contrast,</b>

3864
01:58:41,377 --> 01:58:42,478
<b>momentum contrastive learning.</b>

3865
01:58:42,478 --> 01:58:43,112
<b>Right.</b>

3866
01:58:44,046 --> 01:58:45,915
<b>Even the Chinese name sounds interesting.</b>

3867
01:58:45,915 --> 01:58:46,348
<b>Right, yes.</b>

3868
01:58:46,348 --> 01:58:48,718
<b>Yes, momentum contrastive learning.</b>

3869
01:58:48,718 --> 01:58:49,752
<b>Uh, I think</b>

3870
01:58:49,752 --> 01:58:51,020
<b>you don't need to dig into</b>

3871
01:58:51,020 --> 01:58:52,655
<b>the specific technical details.</b>

3872
01:58:52,655 --> 01:58:53,389
<b>Because now</b>

3873
01:58:53,389 --> 01:58:55,324
<b>much of it is no longer important.</b>

3874
01:58:55,324 --> 01:58:56,392
<b>But in short,</b>

3875
01:58:56,392 --> 01:59:00,529
<b>it was the first to take what's called contrastive learning</b>

3876
01:59:00,529 --> 01:59:01,564
<b>as a framework</b>

3877
01:59:01,564 --> 01:59:04,033
<b>and make it actually work, as a paper.</b>

3878
01:59:05,234 --> 01:59:06,936
<b>And what is contrastive learning?</b>

3879
01:59:06,936 --> 01:59:07,436
<b>Also quite simple.</b>

3880
01:59:07,436 --> 01:59:10,673
<b>We're now in this Representation Space,</b>

3881
01:59:10,673 --> 01:59:12,608
<b>in this representation space,</b>

3882
01:59:12,608 --> 01:59:14,176
<b>there are different points.</b>

3883
01:59:14,810 --> 01:59:17,746
<b>These points may be the same object</b>

3884
01:59:17,913 --> 01:59:19,815
<b>or completely different objects.</b>

3885
01:59:20,182 --> 01:59:21,050
<b>For example,</b>

3886
01:59:21,050 --> 01:59:23,586
<b>I have several images of this chair,</b>

3887
01:59:23,586 --> 01:59:24,453
<b>Right?</b>

3888
01:59:24,453 --> 01:59:26,455
<b>and also some that may be tables,</b>

3889
01:59:26,455 --> 01:59:28,791
<b>or images of cats or dogs.</b>

3890
01:59:29,258 --> 01:59:31,794
<b>These images are all different,</b>

3891
01:59:31,794 --> 01:59:33,229
<b>but in this space,</b>

3892
01:59:33,262 --> 01:59:35,131
<b>we can measure their distances.</b>

3893
01:59:35,331 --> 01:59:36,732
<b>Or we know</b>

3894
01:59:36,732 --> 01:59:38,367
<b>all these different chairs —</b>

3895
01:59:38,534 --> 01:59:41,370
<b>their images should be closer,</b>

3896
01:59:41,370 --> 01:59:43,072
<b>their representations should be closer.</b>

3897
01:59:43,439 --> 01:59:45,774
<b>But a chair and a cat</b>

3898
01:59:45,774 --> 01:59:47,076
<b>should be farther apart.</b>

3899
01:59:47,143 --> 01:59:48,177
<b>Mm-hmm.</b>

3900
01:59:48,177 --> 01:59:49,078
<b>So this is the basic</b>

3901
01:59:49,078 --> 01:59:51,580
<b>logic of contrastive learning.</b>

3902
01:59:51,580 --> 01:59:52,214
<b>And this</b>

3903
01:59:52,214 --> 01:59:53,382
<b>is actually not new.</b>

3904
01:59:53,682 --> 01:59:54,350
<b>This</b>

3905
01:59:54,350 --> 01:59:57,286
<b>It's been done for many, many years.</b>

3906
01:59:57,286 --> 01:59:59,655
<b>By the way, this</b>

3907
01:59:59,655 --> 02:00:00,823
<b>early work</b>

3908
02:00:00,823 --> 02:00:03,692
<b>was actually Yann who first worked with his students</b>

3909
02:00:03,692 --> 02:00:04,793
<b>to do it.</b>

3910
02:00:04,793 --> 02:00:06,295
<b>That's very interesting.</b>

3911
02:00:06,295 --> 02:00:07,429
<b>Of course the problem being solved</b>

3912
02:00:07,429 --> 02:00:09,431
<b>wasn't directly Representation Learning,</b>

3913
02:00:09,465 --> 02:00:11,433
<b>but some Metric Learning problems.</b>

3914
02:00:11,433 --> 02:00:14,069
<b>Some metric learning problems.</b>

3915
02:00:14,069 --> 02:00:15,938
<b>But that's okay.</b>

3916
02:00:15,938 --> 02:00:18,874
<b>This was around 2019,</b>

3917
02:00:18,874 --> 02:00:20,943
<b>I think we gave contrastive learning</b>

3918
02:00:20,943 --> 02:00:21,877
<b>some new meaning.</b>

3919
02:00:22,044 --> 02:00:25,748
<b>But</b>
<b>But this didn't come out of nowhere.</b>

3920
02:00:25,881 --> 02:00:27,082
<b>Actually before that,</b>

3921
02:00:27,249 --> 02:00:29,985
<b>the entire field was slowly moving in this direction,</b>

3922
02:00:29,985 --> 02:00:31,453
<b>expanding.</b>

3923
02:00:31,453 --> 02:00:34,423
<b>For example, there was a paper called CPC,</b>

3924
02:00:34,423 --> 02:00:37,626
<b>and another paper called Memory Bank.</b>

3925
02:00:37,626 --> 02:00:41,063
<b>These two papers were already moving in this direction —</b>

3926
02:00:41,063 --> 02:00:43,966
<b>using contrastive learning to do</b>

3927
02:00:43,966 --> 02:00:46,535
<b>self-supervised learning, having already taken several steps.</b>

3928
02:00:46,669 --> 02:00:48,204
<b>Right, and then</b>

3929
02:00:48,204 --> 02:00:52,808
<b>this is</b>
<b>where I can't help but admire Kaiming's ability.</b>

3930
02:00:52,808 --> 02:00:53,876
<b>I think</b>

3931
02:00:53,876 --> 02:00:56,212
<b>I think this is also</b>

3932
02:00:56,212 --> 02:00:58,147
<b>a moment that made me think, wow,</b>

3933
02:00:58,147 --> 02:01:02,084
<b>what a top-tier researcher</b>

3934
02:01:02,084 --> 02:01:02,585
<b>and</b>

3935
02:01:02,585 --> 02:01:04,687
<b>— or rather, I can't just say top-tier researcher.</b>

3936
02:01:04,687 --> 02:01:06,188
<b>Kaiming in my heart</b>

3937
02:01:06,255 --> 02:01:09,191
<b>is simply the best researcher.</b>

3938
02:01:09,191 --> 02:01:10,826
<b>How does he actually work day-to-day?</b>

3939
02:01:10,826 --> 02:01:11,560
<b>Mm, okay.</b>

3940
02:01:11,560 --> 02:01:12,761
<b>I think there are several points.</b>

3941
02:01:12,761 --> 02:01:14,396
<b>Maybe we can briefly talk about it.</b>

3942
02:01:14,396 --> 02:01:14,697
<b>that is,</b>

3943
02:01:14,697 --> 02:01:20,135
<b>I think he has a kind of extreme focus.</b>

3944
02:01:20,402 --> 02:01:24,406
<b>and</b>
<b>This focus allows him to have a kind of flow state,</b>

3945
02:01:24,406 --> 02:01:25,608
<b>called this kind of mind flow,</b>

3946
02:01:25,608 --> 02:01:29,044
<b>right, he can immerse himself in a problem</b>

3947
02:01:29,211 --> 02:01:31,580
<b>without needing to consider what's happening</b>

3948
02:01:31,580 --> 02:01:32,648
<b>in the rest of the world.</b>

3949
02:01:33,215 --> 02:01:34,016
<b>Mm.</b>

3950
02:01:34,350 --> 02:01:35,551
<b>And I find this particularly</b>

3951
02:01:35,551 --> 02:01:36,819
<b>particularly admirable.</b>

3952
02:01:37,052 --> 02:01:39,955
<b>And another thing is</b>

3953
02:01:39,955 --> 02:01:42,091
<b>how does his focus manifest?</b>

3954
02:01:42,558 --> 02:01:45,027
<b>I think his focus shows in that</b>

3955
02:01:45,861 --> 02:01:46,795
<b>Mm.</b>

3956
02:01:47,463 --> 02:01:49,598
<b>every day, apart from this one problem,</b>

3957
02:01:49,598 --> 02:01:51,333
<b>he won't think about anything else.</b>

3958
02:01:51,800 --> 02:01:54,737
<b>He'll grab the people collaborating with him</b>

3959
02:01:54,737 --> 02:01:55,871
<b>to talk about it,</b>

3960
02:01:55,971 --> 02:01:58,474
<b>and grab other people to talk about it too.</b>

3961
02:01:58,474 --> 02:02:01,410
<b>In any case, this topic is the main subject</b>

3962
02:02:01,410 --> 02:02:02,745
<b>of his thinking.</b>

3963
02:02:02,745 --> 02:02:03,646
<b>Oh.</b>

3964
02:02:03,646 --> 02:02:05,948
<b>And most of his mental cycles</b>

3965
02:02:05,948 --> 02:02:07,049
<b>are allocated to</b>

3966
02:02:07,049 --> 02:02:08,550
<b>this one specific problem.</b>

3967
02:02:08,817 --> 02:02:09,451
<b>Oh.</b>

3968
02:02:09,451 --> 02:02:10,552
<b>This is very difficult.</b>

3969
02:02:10,552 --> 02:02:11,754
<b>I think it's extremely, extremely hard.</b>

3970
02:02:11,754 --> 02:02:12,488
<b>Right, because</b>

3971
02:02:12,488 --> 02:02:14,690
<b>thoughts are often very hard to control.</b>

3972
02:02:14,757 --> 02:02:15,658
<b>Yes, yes, yes.</b>

3973
02:02:15,658 --> 02:02:16,225
<b>Ah right.</b>

3974
02:02:16,225 --> 02:02:18,160
<b>This is related to world models.</b>

3975
02:02:18,160 --> 02:02:19,161
<b>Thoughts are hard to control.</b>

3976
02:02:19,161 --> 02:02:21,230
<b>That's a good point.</b>

3977
02:02:21,230 --> 02:02:22,665
<b>But Kaiming is actually someone very</b>

3978
02:02:22,665 --> 02:02:24,934
<b>capable of this kind of focused decision-making,</b>

3979
02:02:24,934 --> 02:02:26,168
<b>able to concentrate.</b>

3980
02:02:26,168 --> 02:02:26,669
<b>Mm.</b>

3981
02:02:26,669 --> 02:02:28,604
<b>I actually think there are several points.</b>

3982
02:02:28,604 --> 02:02:31,006
<b>I think a top researcher</b>

3983
02:02:31,140 --> 02:02:33,742
<b>needs this ability to varying degrees.</b>

3984
02:02:33,742 --> 02:02:35,844
<b>They need sufficient focus,</b>

3985
02:02:36,245 --> 02:02:37,646
<b>they need good</b>

3986
02:02:37,646 --> 02:02:39,281
<b>research taste.</b>

3987
02:02:39,281 --> 02:02:39,982
<b>How do you define that?</b>

3988
02:02:39,982 --> 02:02:41,016
<b>We can talk about it later.</b>

3989
02:02:41,016 --> 02:02:41,550
<b>Mm.</b>

3990
02:02:41,550 --> 02:02:44,586
<b>And they also need a certain steadfastness —</b>

3991
02:02:44,787 --> 02:02:46,755
<b>you can't just go with the flow</b>

3992
02:02:46,755 --> 02:02:47,556
<b>and</b>

3993
02:02:48,023 --> 02:02:51,393
<b>do what others are interested in.</b>

3994
02:02:51,660 --> 02:02:53,095
<b>And of course</b>

3995
02:02:53,095 --> 02:02:56,999
<b>you also need strong engineering skills,</b>

3996
02:02:57,132 --> 02:02:59,201
<b>research intuition,</b>

3997
02:02:59,201 --> 02:03:01,236
<b>including when you read literature,</b>

3998
02:03:01,236 --> 02:03:02,805
<b>you know what's important</b>

3999
02:03:02,805 --> 02:03:03,772
<b>and what's not.</b>

4000
02:03:03,772 --> 02:03:04,807
<b>This is very important.</b>

4001
02:03:04,807 --> 02:03:06,575
<b>You also know</b>

4002
02:03:06,575 --> 02:03:07,209
<b>that this</b>

4003
02:03:07,209 --> 02:03:08,744
<b>is actually something quite odd</b>

4004
02:03:08,744 --> 02:03:10,212
<b>about academia.</b>

4005
02:03:10,212 --> 02:03:11,013
<b>That is,</b>

4006
02:03:11,013 --> 02:03:12,081
<b>you have to be able to highlight the key points.</b>

4007
02:03:12,281 --> 02:03:13,182
<b>Right.</b>

4008
02:03:13,182 --> 02:03:15,851
<b>The main reason is also that people often don't state them clearly.</b>

4009
02:03:15,851 --> 02:03:18,620
<b>You know?</b>
<b>Sometimes people simply can't articulate the key points,</b>

4010
02:03:18,620 --> 02:03:21,056
<b>sometimes people are unwilling to state them,</b>

4011
02:03:21,056 --> 02:03:21,757
<b>and sometimes</b>

4012
02:03:21,757 --> 02:03:23,959
<b>people haven't realized what the key points are.</b>

4013
02:03:23,959 --> 02:03:26,028
<b>But Kaiming's ability is</b>

4014
02:03:26,028 --> 02:03:29,331
<b>he can peel away the layers</b>

4015
02:03:29,331 --> 02:03:30,065
<b>and extract these key points,</b>

4016
02:03:30,065 --> 02:03:30,933
<b>then tell you,</b>

4017
02:03:31,633 --> 02:03:32,868
<b>and establish</b>

4018
02:03:32,868 --> 02:03:35,337
<b>these connections in this high-dimensional abstract space.</b>

4019
02:03:35,337 --> 02:03:36,038
<b>These connections.</b>

4020
02:03:36,038 --> 02:03:36,672
<b>Oh.</b>

4021
02:03:36,672 --> 02:03:38,240
<b>I find this extremely, extremely impressive.</b>

4022
02:03:38,440 --> 02:03:39,775
<b>Right. So</b>

4023
02:03:39,775 --> 02:03:40,542
<b>many times</b>

4024
02:03:40,542 --> 02:03:42,211
<b>each of Kaiming's ideas</b>

4025
02:03:42,211 --> 02:03:45,381
<b>didn't come from sitting in some corner somewhere,</b>

4026
02:03:45,381 --> 02:03:47,950
<b>dreaming them up at home.</b>

4027
02:03:48,150 --> 02:03:50,185
<b>They actually come</b>

4028
02:03:50,185 --> 02:03:51,854
<b>from constant exploration,</b>

4029
02:03:52,087 --> 02:03:53,756
<b>extensive reading,</b>

4030
02:03:53,856 --> 02:03:55,557
<b>extensive thinking,</b>

4031
02:03:55,557 --> 02:03:57,192
<b>derived little by little.</b>

4032
02:03:57,960 --> 02:03:58,627
<b>And this</b>

4033
02:03:58,627 --> 02:04:01,296
<b>I think truly deeply</b>

4034
02:04:01,296 --> 02:04:01,830
<b>—</b>

4035
02:04:01,830 --> 02:04:03,532
<b>influenced the way I do research,</b>

4036
02:04:03,532 --> 02:04:05,401
<b>and what I now tell my students</b>

4037
02:04:05,401 --> 02:04:07,236
<b>about how research should be done.</b>

4038
02:04:07,236 --> 02:04:08,670
<b>It's about increasing input.</b>

4039
02:04:08,737 --> 02:04:10,005
<b>Increasing input.</b>

4040
02:04:10,005 --> 02:04:10,572
<b>And</b>

4041
02:04:10,572 --> 02:04:12,574
<b>I think</b>
<b>there's actually a paradigm here.</b>

4042
02:04:12,574 --> 02:04:13,208
<b>Mm, in this,</b>

4043
02:04:13,208 --> 02:04:15,244
<b>this paradigm is also something Kaiming taught me.</b>

4044
02:04:15,244 --> 02:04:16,211
<b>Right, he said</b>

4045
02:04:16,412 --> 02:04:18,847
<b>actually all these ideas</b>

4046
02:04:18,881 --> 02:04:21,016
<b>you can't just sit there and think up,</b>

4047
02:04:21,016 --> 02:04:23,051
<b>because if you want to come up with an idea</b>

4048
02:04:23,619 --> 02:04:24,420
<b>Mm.</b>

4049
02:04:24,653 --> 02:04:26,755
<b>by just thinking, it's definitely not a good idea.</b>

4050
02:04:27,056 --> 02:04:29,758
<b>There are really only a few possibilities.</b>

4051
02:04:29,758 --> 02:04:32,161
<b>The first possibility:</b>

4052
02:04:32,161 --> 02:04:33,896
<b>you're smarter than everyone else in the world,</b>

4053
02:04:33,896 --> 02:04:36,265
<b>so</b>
<b>you come up with an incredibly brilliant idea</b>

4054
02:04:36,265 --> 02:04:38,534
<b>that no one else can think of.</b>

4055
02:04:38,534 --> 02:04:40,269
<b>But I think the probability of this is extremely small.</b>

4056
02:04:40,636 --> 02:04:43,405
<b>So the more likely two possibilities: first,</b>

4057
02:04:43,405 --> 02:04:45,240
<b>while you're thinking of this idea,</b>

4058
02:04:45,240 --> 02:04:46,442
<b>100 people,</b>

4059
02:04:46,442 --> 02:04:47,142
<b>1000 people,</b>

4060
02:04:47,142 --> 02:04:48,977
<b>10,000 people in the world are thinking the same idea.</b>

4061
02:04:49,244 --> 02:04:51,046
<b>So you'll have to compete with them,</b>

4062
02:04:51,380 --> 02:04:53,248
<b>and your execution speed may not be faster than theirs.</b>

4063
02:04:53,649 --> 02:04:55,284
<b>The second possibility:</b>

4064
02:04:55,451 --> 02:04:56,985
<b>this is a very bad idea</b>

4065
02:04:57,152 --> 02:04:59,054
<b>that others have already tried many times</b>

4066
02:04:59,188 --> 02:04:59,788
<b>unsuccessfully.</b>

4067
02:04:59,788 --> 02:05:00,589
<b>unsuccessfully.</b>

4068
02:05:00,656 --> 02:05:01,356
<b>Mm.</b>

4069
02:05:01,356 --> 02:05:02,825
<b>Then you probably don't need to try either.</b>

4070
02:05:02,825 --> 02:05:03,992
<b>Mm. So</b>

4071
02:05:03,992 --> 02:05:06,061
<b>So I think Kaiming's greatest influence on me is</b>

4072
02:05:06,061 --> 02:05:09,364
<b>he taught me how to find a research idea.</b>

4073
02:05:09,364 --> 02:05:10,365
<b>Mm. How?</b>

4074
02:05:10,566 --> 02:05:12,267
<b>I think this is a process of seeking.</b>

4075
02:05:12,668 --> 02:05:13,635
<b>so</b>

4076
02:05:13,635 --> 02:05:14,636
<b>Now I,</b>

4077
02:05:14,636 --> 02:05:15,571
<b>when new students come in,</b>

4078
02:05:15,571 --> 02:05:16,071
<b>I will tell everyone</b>

4079
02:05:16,071 --> 02:05:19,174
<b>about a research cycle.</b>

4080
02:05:19,408 --> 02:05:21,243
<b>Uh, of course I hope it could be longer,</b>

4081
02:05:21,243 --> 02:05:23,745
<b>but in today's competitive environment,</b>

4082
02:05:24,046 --> 02:05:25,647
<b>there might be at most 6 months.</b>

4083
02:05:26,849 --> 02:05:27,783
<b>That is,</b>

4084
02:05:27,816 --> 02:05:29,251
<b>from the beginning of 6 months,</b>

4085
02:05:29,251 --> 02:05:30,786
<b>you need to start thinking about an idea,</b>

4086
02:05:31,153 --> 02:05:32,421
<b>and then later</b>

4087
02:05:32,421 --> 02:05:34,890
<b>you need to write this idea into a paper</b>

4088
02:05:35,023 --> 02:05:36,091
<b>and publish it.</b>

4089
02:05:36,225 --> 02:05:38,494
<b>This whole cycle is about 6 months.</b>

4090
02:05:40,762 --> 02:05:42,431
<b>What does this process look like?</b>

4091
02:05:42,564 --> 02:05:44,366
<b>You need to have a general direction,</b>

4092
02:05:44,366 --> 02:05:45,267
<b>you need to know what you want to do.</b>

4093
02:05:45,267 --> 02:05:47,069
<b>You can't know nothing at all</b>

4094
02:05:47,069 --> 02:05:48,804
<b>just saying "I want to do research" isn't enough.</b>

4095
02:05:48,871 --> 02:05:49,638
<b>This</b>

4096
02:05:49,638 --> 02:05:52,841
<b>can be achieved by talking with your advisor,</b>

4097
02:05:52,975 --> 02:05:54,943
<b>or with your peers,</b>

4098
02:05:54,943 --> 02:05:56,945
<b>discussing with your classmates,</b>

4099
02:05:57,012 --> 02:05:58,881
<b>or through your own reading,</b>

4100
02:05:58,981 --> 02:06:00,883
<b>developing some general direction,</b>

4101
02:06:00,883 --> 02:06:02,818
<b>this directional understanding.</b>

4102
02:06:02,818 --> 02:06:03,819
<b>Mm, right?</b>

4103
02:06:04,186 --> 02:06:05,053
<b>But</b>

4104
02:06:05,053 --> 02:06:09,825
<b>you must give yourself enough time and space</b>

4105
02:06:10,158 --> 02:06:12,227
<b>to explore.</b>

4106
02:06:12,361 --> 02:06:13,395
<b>And this exploration,</b>

4107
02:06:13,395 --> 02:06:14,863
<b>this exploration phase,</b>

4108
02:06:14,963 --> 02:06:15,330
<b>I think</b>

4109
02:06:15,330 --> 02:06:17,366
<b>should last at least one to two months.</b>

4110
02:06:17,432 --> 02:06:18,834
<b>What should you do during the exploration phase?</b>

4111
02:06:19,067 --> 02:06:19,635
<b>The exploration phase —</b>

4112
02:06:19,635 --> 02:06:21,436
<b>good question. What do you do during exploration?</b>

4113
02:06:21,570 --> 02:06:23,338
<b>You can't just sit there thinking.</b>

4114
02:06:23,672 --> 02:06:24,540
<b>What you need to explore is</b>

4115
02:06:24,540 --> 02:06:27,809
<b>constantly hacking things,</b>

4116
02:06:27,809 --> 02:06:29,044
<b>ah,</b>

4117
02:06:29,177 --> 02:06:29,845
<b>that is,</b>

4118
02:06:29,845 --> 02:06:32,748
<b>you really have to be like a hacker,</b>

4119
02:06:32,748 --> 02:06:34,416
<b>playing with things,</b>

4120
02:06:34,416 --> 02:06:36,251
<b>messing around with things.</b>

4121
02:06:36,251 --> 02:06:38,287
<b>Treat research like a game,</b>

4122
02:06:38,287 --> 02:06:39,555
<b>like a toy to play with.</b>

4123
02:06:39,555 --> 02:06:41,056
<b>Mm, this might involve, for example,</b>

4124
02:06:41,056 --> 02:06:42,691
<b>working through formulas,</b>

4125
02:06:42,758 --> 02:06:44,192
<b>reading more papers,</b>

4126
02:06:44,192 --> 02:06:45,961
<b>finding some connections,</b>

4127
02:06:45,961 --> 02:06:46,295
<b>of course,</b>

4128
02:06:46,295 --> 02:06:49,998
<b>and perhaps more importantly, actually doing things,</b>

4129
02:06:50,465 --> 02:06:52,801
<b>writing code.</b>

4130
02:06:52,968 --> 02:06:54,970
<b>But when you're writing code,</b>

4131
02:06:55,270 --> 02:06:57,005
<b>what you need to note is</b>

4132
02:06:57,005 --> 02:06:57,639
<b>the code you write</b>

4133
02:06:57,639 --> 02:07:00,809
<b>is not your initial starting idea</b>

4134
02:07:00,809 --> 02:07:03,011
<b>or direction,</b>

4135
02:07:03,011 --> 02:07:04,680
<b>but an exploration process.</b>

4136
02:07:04,680 --> 02:07:05,447
<b>So the code you write</b>

4137
02:07:05,447 --> 02:07:07,849
<b>might simply reproduce a baseline,</b>

4138
02:07:08,350 --> 02:07:10,786
<b>take what someone else's paper is doing</b>

4139
02:07:10,786 --> 02:07:13,221
<b>and reproduce it.</b>

4140
02:07:13,589 --> 02:07:14,590
<b>and</b>

4141
02:07:14,590 --> 02:07:17,159
<b>And it might also be on the basis of this baseline</b>

4142
02:07:17,159 --> 02:07:19,027
<b>to make some kind of extension.</b>

4143
02:07:19,027 --> 02:07:19,828
<b>Mm.</b>

4144
02:07:19,861 --> 02:07:21,430
<b>And the most important thing in all this</b>

4145
02:07:21,430 --> 02:07:23,432
<b>is to find a signal.</b>

4146
02:07:23,432 --> 02:07:23,765
<b>that is,</b>

4147
02:07:23,765 --> 02:07:26,635
<b>it's still a bit like what you just said —</b>

4148
02:07:26,635 --> 02:07:28,337
<b>all of this decision-making process</b>

4149
02:07:28,537 --> 02:07:31,073
<b>is actually a quite disorderly exploration process.</b>

4150
02:07:31,673 --> 02:07:32,641
<b>It's a</b>

4151
02:07:32,641 --> 02:07:34,142
<b>what we call stochastic gradient descent.</b>

4152
02:07:34,142 --> 02:07:35,544
<b>Right?</b>

4153
02:07:35,644 --> 02:07:38,380
<b>This is a cornerstone of all machine learning,</b>

4154
02:07:38,380 --> 02:07:42,484
<b>but it equally applies to research itself</b>

4155
02:07:42,484 --> 02:07:43,752
<b>and to our lives.</b>

4156
02:07:43,785 --> 02:07:44,253
<b>that is,</b>

4157
02:07:44,253 --> 02:07:46,355
<b>In everyone's pursuit of their ultimate goal,</b>

4158
02:07:46,355 --> 02:07:48,390
<b>they're all going through a stochastic</b>

4159
02:07:48,390 --> 02:07:49,691
<b>gradient descent process.</b>

4160
02:07:49,691 --> 02:07:50,592
<b>Mm.</b>

4161
02:07:50,792 --> 02:07:52,894
<b>And I think research is the same.</b>

4162
02:07:52,961 --> 02:07:53,629
<b>For you,</b>

4163
02:07:53,629 --> 02:07:55,163
<b>the most important thing in research</b>

4164
02:07:55,597 --> 02:07:58,533
<b>is not going from point A to point B.</b>

4165
02:07:58,533 --> 02:07:59,534
<b>For example, A is an idea,</b>

4166
02:07:59,534 --> 02:08:00,469
<b>B is a paper,</b>

4167
02:08:00,636 --> 02:08:02,204
<b>but rather in this process,</b>

4168
02:08:02,271 --> 02:08:04,006
<b>what kind of signal can you find?</b>

4169
02:08:04,206 --> 02:08:04,940
<b>Your gradient,</b>

4170
02:08:04,940 --> 02:08:06,208
<b>where exactly is your gradient?</b>

4171
02:08:06,475 --> 02:08:08,010
<b>Right. So</b>

4172
02:08:08,410 --> 02:08:10,812
<b>Kaiming's view is</b>

4173
02:08:10,812 --> 02:08:12,281
<b>this gradient itself</b>

4174
02:08:12,281 --> 02:08:15,984
<b>is the source of your real idea.</b>

4175
02:08:16,752 --> 02:08:18,453
<b>When you've gone through constant exploration,</b>

4176
02:08:18,453 --> 02:08:20,255
<b>tried many things,</b>

4177
02:08:20,255 --> 02:08:21,223
<b>possibly unsuccessful,</b>

4178
02:08:21,223 --> 02:08:21,890
<b>possibly successful,</b>

4179
02:08:21,890 --> 02:08:24,092
<b>by the way, it doesn't have to be a successful experiment</b>

4180
02:08:24,092 --> 02:08:25,027
<b>to give you this gradient.</b>

4181
02:08:25,027 --> 02:08:26,561
<b>Sometimes a failed experiment</b>

4182
02:08:26,561 --> 02:08:27,996
<b>gives you a larger gradient.</b>

4183
02:08:28,130 --> 02:08:29,131
<b>Right?</b>

4184
02:08:29,131 --> 02:08:30,599
<b>That is, as long as</b>

4185
02:08:30,599 --> 02:08:32,534
<b>the most feared thing is not knowing which direction to go.</b>

4186
02:08:32,534 --> 02:08:33,335
<b>Mm.</b>

4187
02:08:33,335 --> 02:08:34,670
<b>So a good result,</b>

4188
02:08:34,670 --> 02:08:36,004
<b>a bad result,</b>

4189
02:08:36,004 --> 02:08:37,072
<b>are both good results.</b>

4190
02:08:37,072 --> 02:08:38,373
<b>For research,</b>

4191
02:08:38,540 --> 02:08:39,408
<b>a surprise,</b>

4192
02:08:39,408 --> 02:08:41,376
<b>something surprising,</b>

4193
02:08:41,376 --> 02:08:42,577
<b>such an observation,</b>

4194
02:08:42,577 --> 02:08:44,413
<b>is always for a researcher</b>

4195
02:08:44,413 --> 02:08:45,947
<b>— for a researcher —</b>

4196
02:08:46,048 --> 02:08:47,215
<b>the most joyful thing.</b>

4197
02:08:47,215 --> 02:08:48,550
<b>Something unexpected that you observed.</b>

4198
02:08:48,550 --> 02:08:50,385
<b>Right.</b>
<b>You saw something unexpected.</b>

4199
02:08:50,385 --> 02:08:51,186
<b>Mm.</b>

4200
02:08:51,787 --> 02:08:53,255
<b>so</b>

4201
02:08:53,255 --> 02:08:53,955
<b>he said,</b>

4202
02:08:53,955 --> 02:08:55,757
<b>It's after this kind of exploration</b>

4203
02:08:55,757 --> 02:08:57,259
<b>In this process,</b>

4204
02:08:57,426 --> 02:08:58,960
<b>the ideas you discover</b>

4205
02:08:58,960 --> 02:09:00,696
<b>are the truly your own ideas.</b>

4206
02:09:01,630 --> 02:09:04,032
<b>The idea you started with isn't your idea.</b>

4207
02:09:04,066 --> 02:09:05,233
<b>That thing doesn't belong to you.</b>

4208
02:09:05,434 --> 02:09:07,636
<b>The idea found in exploration is your own idea.</b>

4209
02:09:07,936 --> 02:09:09,371
<b>And the research process</b>

4210
02:09:09,438 --> 02:09:11,973
<b>is about finding</b>

4211
02:09:11,973 --> 02:09:13,375
<b>your own idea.</b>

4212
02:09:13,542 --> 02:09:14,242
<b>But</b>

4213
02:09:14,242 --> 02:09:15,377
<b>this word,</b>

4214
02:09:15,744 --> 02:09:16,845
<b>you need to see</b>

4215
02:09:16,845 --> 02:09:18,346
<b>it belongs to — this thing</b>

4216
02:09:18,346 --> 02:09:20,349
<b>is truly your own.</b>

4217
02:09:20,349 --> 02:09:22,050
<b>Like heaven gave you an inspiration,</b>

4218
02:09:22,084 --> 02:09:23,251
<b>injected it into your head.</b>

4219
02:09:23,251 --> 02:09:25,287
<b>Right, on one hand heaven gives you inspiration,</b>

4220
02:09:25,287 --> 02:09:25,887
<b>on the other hand,</b>

4221
02:09:25,887 --> 02:09:29,157
<b>it's also based on extensive empirical work and practice.</b>

4222
02:09:29,157 --> 02:09:30,292
<b>Right?</b>

4223
02:09:30,292 --> 02:09:32,427
<b>There's no free lunch here.</b>

4224
02:09:32,427 --> 02:09:33,695
<b>Maybe you're truly a genius,</b>

4225
02:09:33,695 --> 02:09:36,198
<b>or maybe you're extremely lucky,</b>

4226
02:09:36,331 --> 02:09:37,532
<b>God holding your hand</b>

4227
02:09:37,532 --> 02:09:38,767
<b>wrote this formula.</b>

4228
02:09:38,934 --> 02:09:40,335
<b>It can happen.</b>

4229
02:09:40,335 --> 02:09:42,804
<b>But most of the time, most progress,</b>

4230
02:09:42,838 --> 02:09:44,806
<b>even most work that has great</b>

4231
02:09:44,806 --> 02:09:46,808
<b>influence on the field,</b>

4232
02:09:46,808 --> 02:09:50,345
<b>I think still happens step by step.</b>

4233
02:09:50,379 --> 02:09:52,781
<b>You can always trace back</b>

4234
02:09:52,781 --> 02:09:54,549
<b>to find its starting point.</b>

4235
02:09:54,549 --> 02:09:56,218
<b>So I also tell students</b>

4236
02:09:56,418 --> 02:09:59,187
<b>what's actually the worst kind of research?</b>

4237
02:09:59,287 --> 02:10:02,691
<b>It's when you define a problem at the start,</b>

4238
02:10:02,824 --> 02:10:04,192
<b>say this is my idea,</b>

4239
02:10:04,593 --> 02:10:06,027
<b>and in the end publish a paper</b>

4240
02:10:06,461 --> 02:10:07,629
<b>whose idea</b>

4241
02:10:07,629 --> 02:10:09,765
<b>is exactly the same as what you started with.</b>

4242
02:10:10,198 --> 02:10:11,633
<b>You didn't encounter any obstacles,</b>

4243
02:10:12,000 --> 02:10:13,402
<b>you didn't encounter any difficulties.</b>

4244
02:10:14,069 --> 02:10:15,270
<b>Why is it the worst?</b>

4245
02:10:15,437 --> 02:10:16,471
<b>Because this shows</b>

4246
02:10:16,471 --> 02:10:18,540
<b>your idea is a boring idea,</b>

4247
02:10:18,673 --> 02:10:21,343
<b>and your published paper is a boring paper.</b>

4248
02:10:21,576 --> 02:10:22,644
<b>Right.</b>

4249
02:10:22,644 --> 02:10:24,946
<b>I think</b>

4250
02:10:24,946 --> 02:10:26,081
<b>after many years of observation,</b>

4251
02:10:26,081 --> 02:10:27,582
<b>this is indeed very, very accurate.</b>

4252
02:10:27,649 --> 02:10:29,484
<b>So I think this is also why</b>

4253
02:10:29,484 --> 02:10:30,485
<b>I tell students this —</b>

4254
02:10:30,485 --> 02:10:30,852
<b>because</b>

4255
02:10:30,852 --> 02:10:33,655
<b>people sometimes can't accept this fact.</b>

4256
02:10:33,655 --> 02:10:34,823
<b>People always think</b>

4257
02:10:35,190 --> 02:10:38,193
<b>I should start by thinking of a clever trick,</b>

4258
02:10:38,360 --> 02:10:40,162
<b>then implement it,</b>

4259
02:10:40,162 --> 02:10:41,062
<b>make it work,</b>

4260
02:10:41,062 --> 02:10:41,997
<b>publish a paper,</b>

4261
02:10:41,997 --> 02:10:42,597
<b>I've succeeded,</b>

4262
02:10:42,597 --> 02:10:45,033
<b>and I move on to the next thing.</b>

4263
02:10:45,033 --> 02:10:47,269
<b>But what this can give for personal accumulation</b>

4264
02:10:47,269 --> 02:10:48,637
<b>is actually very, very limited.</b>

4265
02:10:49,004 --> 02:10:50,772
<b>The exploration process is actually very difficult.</b>

4266
02:10:50,806 --> 02:10:52,374
<b>Many people don't know how to explore.</b>

4267
02:10:52,374 --> 02:10:53,141
<b>Exploration is very hard.</b>

4268
02:10:53,141 --> 02:10:54,476
<b>And this is why</b>

4269
02:10:54,476 --> 02:10:57,379
<b>all these papers in my view are nonlinear.</b>

4270
02:10:57,379 --> 02:10:59,181
<b>This nonlinearity shows in two aspects.</b>

4271
02:10:59,414 --> 02:11:03,618
<b>The first is your 6 months of time —</b>

4272
02:11:03,685 --> 02:11:06,087
<b>by the 5th month,</b>

4273
02:11:06,087 --> 02:11:07,255
<b>like I just told you,</b>

4274
02:11:07,255 --> 02:11:08,023
<b>your mindset collapses.</b>

4275
02:11:08,023 --> 02:11:09,624
<b>This ResNeXt story —</b>

4276
02:11:09,624 --> 02:11:11,626
<b>on one hand people hear, wow,</b>

4277
02:11:11,626 --> 02:11:13,662
<b>you changed direction in the last month</b>

4278
02:11:13,662 --> 02:11:15,897
<b>and made it work.</b>

4279
02:11:15,897 --> 02:11:18,033
<b>That time period is so short,</b>

4280
02:11:18,033 --> 02:11:19,434
<b>and you still managed to do it.</b>

4281
02:11:19,468 --> 02:11:21,002
<b>It sounds unbelievable.</b>

4282
02:11:21,469 --> 02:11:23,438
<b>But once you know this happens too often,</b>

4283
02:11:23,438 --> 02:11:25,574
<b>you find there really is a pattern.</b>

4284
02:11:25,574 --> 02:11:26,541
<b>You often go through this.</b>

4285
02:11:26,541 --> 02:11:27,209
<b>I often go through this.</b>

4286
02:11:27,209 --> 02:11:29,478
<b>Or rather, my best work always happens this way.</b>

4287
02:11:29,845 --> 02:11:32,080
<b>So how do you maintain your mindset for the first 5 months?</b>

4288
02:11:32,147 --> 02:11:33,281
<b>Uh, there's no way around it.</b>

4289
02:11:33,281 --> 02:11:35,350
<b>You have to accept this fact,</b>

4290
02:11:35,350 --> 02:11:39,054
<b>you have to be able to tell yourself</b>

4291
02:11:39,054 --> 02:11:42,023
<b>this is a normal research process.</b>

4292
02:11:42,023 --> 02:11:44,960
<b>Would you consider switching direction in the first 5 months?</b>

4293
02:11:44,960 --> 02:11:47,762
<b>I might go for that boring idea.</b>

4294
02:11:47,762 --> 02:11:48,430
<b>I think you would.</b>

4295
02:11:48,430 --> 02:11:50,232
<b>And</b>

4296
02:11:50,232 --> 02:11:52,434
<b>changing direction is actually very, very important.</b>

4297
02:11:52,467 --> 02:11:54,169
<b>You must learn to pivot.</b>

4298
02:11:54,169 --> 02:11:54,769
<b>Because I just said,</b>

4299
02:11:54,769 --> 02:11:55,570
<b>the worst work is</b>

4300
02:11:55,570 --> 02:11:57,172
<b>when your starting idea is the same idea</b>

4301
02:11:57,172 --> 02:11:58,440
<b>as your ending idea.</b>

4302
02:11:58,440 --> 02:12:00,041
<b>The best work is</b>

4303
02:12:00,208 --> 02:12:03,645
<b>when you've gone all around,</b>

4304
02:12:03,645 --> 02:12:05,213
<b>jumping here and there,</b>

4305
02:12:05,213 --> 02:12:06,882
<b>taken a long, winding road,</b>

4306
02:12:06,882 --> 02:12:08,016
<b>and only then arrived at this point.</b>

4307
02:12:08,016 --> 02:12:08,750
<b>Mm.</b>

4308
02:12:08,750 --> 02:12:11,086
<b>Though this road is very bumpy,</b>

4309
02:12:11,086 --> 02:12:13,788
<b>from the final destination</b>

4310
02:12:13,788 --> 02:12:14,823
<b>step by step</b>

4311
02:12:14,823 --> 02:12:16,625
<b>you can always trace back to the very beginning.</b>

4312
02:12:16,625 --> 02:12:18,226
<b>Only then can it be connected into a line.</b>

4313
02:12:18,226 --> 02:12:18,627
<b>Only then can you</b>

4314
02:12:18,627 --> 02:12:20,262
<b>but during the process, you can't.</b>

4315
02:12:20,395 --> 02:12:22,197
<b>Yes, during the process</b>

4316
02:12:22,197 --> 02:12:22,731
<b>I think</b>

4317
02:12:22,731 --> 02:12:23,665
<b>you're in the process —</b>

4318
02:12:23,665 --> 02:12:24,332
<b>because you don't know,</b>

4319
02:12:24,332 --> 02:12:25,166
<b>you can't predict the future.</b>

4320
02:12:25,166 --> 02:12:27,469
<b>So this is always an exploration process.</b>

4321
02:12:27,636 --> 02:12:30,472
<b>So I think about two months of exploration,</b>

4322
02:12:30,472 --> 02:12:32,207
<b>gradually forming an idea,</b>

4323
02:12:32,374 --> 02:12:33,975
<b>then gradually expanding,</b>

4324
02:12:33,975 --> 02:12:35,610
<b>then scaling up,</b>

4325
02:12:35,610 --> 02:12:36,545
<b>Right?</b>

4326
02:12:36,545 --> 02:12:37,846
<b>then supplementing experiments sufficiently,</b>

4327
02:12:37,846 --> 02:12:38,246
<b>This thing,</b>

4328
02:12:38,246 --> 02:12:40,582
<b>might take another two to three months,</b>

4329
02:12:40,582 --> 02:12:41,349
<b>and finally writing the paper</b>

4330
02:12:41,349 --> 02:12:43,385
<b>— then spending one to two months —</b>

4331
02:12:43,418 --> 02:12:43,952
<b>this is</b>

4332
02:12:43,952 --> 02:12:44,986
<b>already a very</b>

4333
02:12:44,986 --> 02:12:47,188
<b>smooth research process.</b>

4334
02:12:47,188 --> 02:12:48,156
<b>Mm.</b>

4335
02:12:48,290 --> 02:12:49,391
<b>And I think</b>

4336
02:12:49,391 --> 02:12:51,793
<b>this, again,</b>

4337
02:12:51,793 --> 02:12:52,894
<b>in today's era,</b>

4338
02:12:52,894 --> 02:12:54,863
<b>faces many, many challenges.</b>

4339
02:12:54,863 --> 02:12:57,532
<b>People face all kinds of pressure.</b>

4340
02:12:57,532 --> 02:12:59,801
<b>Right? I think the competitive pressure now is too great.</b>

4341
02:12:59,801 --> 02:13:01,236
<b>The competitive pressure is too great.</b>

4342
02:13:01,236 --> 02:13:02,003
<b>and</b>

4343
02:13:02,003 --> 02:13:02,804
<b>I think</b>

4344
02:13:02,804 --> 02:13:04,139
<b>It makes people feel</b>

4345
02:13:04,339 --> 02:13:07,609
<b>they must chase the cutting edge</b>

4346
02:13:07,609 --> 02:13:09,811
<b>and finish things as soon as possible,</b>

4347
02:13:09,811 --> 02:13:10,879
<b>seize the opportunity.</b>

4348
02:13:10,879 --> 02:13:11,546
<b>Mm.</b>

4349
02:13:11,546 --> 02:13:12,614
<b>Claim the territory.</b>

4350
02:13:12,814 --> 02:13:15,250
<b>but looking back,</b>

4351
02:13:15,250 --> 02:13:17,252
<b>I think, as I just said,</b>

4352
02:13:17,252 --> 02:13:19,955
<b>Professor Fei-Fei's greatest strength</b>

4353
02:13:20,555 --> 02:13:24,192
<b>is</b>
<b>that she's someone who can define problems.</b>

4354
02:13:24,192 --> 02:13:26,361
<b>If you lose the ability to define problems,</b>

4355
02:13:26,594 --> 02:13:28,964
<b>you essentially also lose much of the ability to innovate,</b>

4356
02:13:28,964 --> 02:13:31,666
<b>essentially also lose the ability to do research.</b>

4357
02:13:31,933 --> 02:13:33,602
<b>And this</b>

4358
02:13:33,602 --> 02:13:36,671
<b>I just said research is nonlinear,</b>

4359
02:13:36,671 --> 02:13:38,073
<b>that's in terms of time.</b>

4360
02:13:38,139 --> 02:13:39,374
<b>But in terms of results,</b>

4361
02:13:39,374 --> 02:13:40,542
<b>it's also nonlinear.</b>

4362
02:13:40,542 --> 02:13:41,343
<b>Mm.</b>

4363
02:13:41,443 --> 02:13:43,011
<b>That is, this</b>

4364
02:13:43,011 --> 02:13:45,246
<b>is actually MIT professor Bill Freeman —</b>

4365
02:13:45,246 --> 02:13:47,415
<b>he has a very classic</b>

4366
02:13:47,415 --> 02:13:48,216
<b>plot,</b>

4367
02:13:48,216 --> 02:13:51,386
<b>an illustration,</b>

4368
02:13:51,386 --> 02:13:52,187
<b>this kind of graphic.</b>

4369
02:13:52,187 --> 02:13:54,389
<b>He often talks about it when giving talks.</b>

4370
02:13:54,856 --> 02:13:55,857
<b>So,</b>

4371
02:13:55,857 --> 02:13:57,826
<b>This graphic has a horizontal axis</b>

4372
02:13:57,826 --> 02:13:58,994
<b>and a vertical axis.</b>

4373
02:13:59,461 --> 02:14:02,597
<b>The horizontal axis starts from a very poor work,</b>

4374
02:14:02,998 --> 02:14:04,366
<b>a decent work,</b>

4375
02:14:04,566 --> 02:14:05,834
<b>a very good work,</b>

4376
02:14:06,167 --> 02:14:08,003
<b>an exceptionally impressive work.</b>

4377
02:14:08,003 --> 02:14:09,037
<b>This is the horizontal axis.</b>

4378
02:14:09,804 --> 02:14:10,538
<b>The vertical axis</b>

4379
02:14:10,538 --> 02:14:14,009
<b>is the impact on your entire career.</b>

4380
02:14:14,009 --> 02:14:16,044
<b>The impact of this paper on your career.</b>

4381
02:14:16,945 --> 02:14:17,879
<b>So you can guess</b>

4382
02:14:17,879 --> 02:14:19,581
<b>what this curve actually looks like.</b>

4383
02:14:19,581 --> 02:14:21,182
<b>Right? It's not a linear curve.</b>

4384
02:14:21,483 --> 02:14:24,252
<b>It's not that a very poor work</b>

4385
02:14:24,252 --> 02:14:26,655
<b>has a very bad career impact,</b>

4386
02:14:26,855 --> 02:14:27,555
<b>and</b>

4387
02:14:27,555 --> 02:14:28,690
<b>and the best work</b>

4388
02:14:28,690 --> 02:14:30,291
<b>or a fairly good work</b>

4389
02:14:30,291 --> 02:14:32,394
<b>gives you a very good return,</b>

4390
02:14:32,394 --> 02:14:33,361
<b>gradually increasing.</b>

4391
02:14:33,361 --> 02:14:34,162
<b>It's not linear.</b>

4392
02:14:34,162 --> 02:14:35,063
<b>It's not linear.</b>

4393
02:14:35,230 --> 02:14:36,331
<b>It's saying</b>

4394
02:14:36,965 --> 02:14:38,633
<b>basically, a very poor work</b>

4395
02:14:38,633 --> 02:14:40,201
<b>actually won't hurt you much,</b>

4396
02:14:40,201 --> 02:14:41,569
<b>nobody cares.</b>

4397
02:14:41,569 --> 02:14:41,936
<b>Mm.</b>

4398
02:14:41,936 --> 02:14:42,971
<b>No one will notice.</b>

4399
02:14:43,571 --> 02:14:44,806
<b>A decent work —</b>

4400
02:14:44,806 --> 02:14:45,874
<b>no one notices either.</b>

4401
02:14:45,874 --> 02:14:47,409
<b>The gains it brings you are also small.</b>

4402
02:14:47,409 --> 02:14:48,209
<b>Mm.</b>

4403
02:14:48,843 --> 02:14:49,644
<b>But sometimes,</b>

4404
02:14:49,644 --> 02:14:52,580
<b>when you produce a very good piece of work,</b>

4405
02:14:52,981 --> 02:14:53,982
<b>an exceptionally impressive work,</b>

4406
02:14:53,982 --> 02:14:55,817
<b>work that everyone knows about,</b>

4407
02:14:55,850 --> 02:14:57,685
<b>your impact</b>

4408
02:14:57,685 --> 02:14:59,587
<b>— I said I don't like the word impact —</b>

4409
02:14:59,587 --> 02:15:00,622
<b>reaches the top.</b>

4410
02:15:00,889 --> 02:15:01,456
<b>This thing,</b>

4411
02:15:01,456 --> 02:15:02,190
<b>this,</b>

4412
02:15:02,190 --> 02:15:03,591
<b>immediately shoots up to</b>

4413
02:15:03,591 --> 02:15:04,259
<b>the top.</b>

4414
02:15:04,259 --> 02:15:04,826
<b>Right?</b>

4415
02:15:04,826 --> 02:15:06,461
<b>So we often say in academia</b>

4416
02:15:06,461 --> 02:15:08,430
<b>what people measure is the so-called signature work.</b>

4417
02:15:08,830 --> 02:15:10,131
<b>Or another way to put it:</b>

4418
02:15:10,131 --> 02:15:10,865
<b>people say</b>

4419
02:15:10,865 --> 02:15:14,402
<b>what you optimize for is not an average —</b>

4420
02:15:14,402 --> 02:15:16,171
<b>not the average of all your previous work —</b>

4421
02:15:16,171 --> 02:15:17,739
<b>an average.</b>

4422
02:15:17,739 --> 02:15:19,040
<b>But</b>

4423
02:15:19,040 --> 02:15:20,775
<b>what you're optimizing is</b>

4424
02:15:20,775 --> 02:15:22,210
<b>the maximum of your work.</b>

4425
02:15:22,210 --> 02:15:24,379
<b>Right, the highest point.</b>

4426
02:15:24,813 --> 02:15:27,282
<b>I think this illustrates</b>

4427
02:15:27,282 --> 02:15:29,384
<b>the research game's</b>

4428
02:15:29,384 --> 02:15:31,219
<b>nonlinear characteristic.</b>

4429
02:15:31,219 --> 02:15:31,986
<b>Mm.</b>

4430
02:15:31,986 --> 02:15:33,488
<b>So is the highest point good or not?</b>

4431
02:15:33,488 --> 02:15:34,289
<b>Of course it's good!</b>

4432
02:15:34,289 --> 02:15:35,090
<b>that is,</b>

4433
02:15:35,090 --> 02:15:35,557
<b>You</b>

4434
02:15:35,557 --> 02:15:36,424
<b>you only need to</b>

4435
02:15:36,424 --> 02:15:38,460
<b>succeed just once in your lifetime.</b>

4436
02:15:38,460 --> 02:15:39,861
<b>And this</b>

4437
02:15:39,861 --> 02:15:42,397
<b>I actually gave a talk about this at CVPR,</b>

4438
02:15:42,397 --> 02:15:44,332
<b>I called it research: the infinite game.</b>

4439
02:15:44,332 --> 02:15:44,766
<b>Mm, right?</b>

4440
02:15:44,766 --> 02:15:45,734
<b>This</b>

4441
02:15:45,734 --> 02:15:47,035
<b>got quite a strong response from everyone.</b>

4442
02:15:47,035 --> 02:15:49,237
<b>I think actually</b>

4443
02:15:49,237 --> 02:15:51,272
<b>I rarely give these non-technical</b>

4444
02:15:51,272 --> 02:15:52,073
<b>talks,</b>

4445
02:15:52,073 --> 02:15:54,409
<b>because this is more about philosophical thinking</b>

4446
02:15:54,409 --> 02:15:55,744
<b>and some summaries.</b>

4447
02:15:55,744 --> 02:15:57,245
<b>That one was actually quite good.</b>

4448
02:15:57,412 --> 02:15:59,280
<b>and</b>

4449
02:15:59,280 --> 02:16:01,382
<b>But it also</b>

4450
02:16:01,382 --> 02:16:03,752
<b>contained everything I talked about above.</b>

4451
02:16:03,752 --> 02:16:05,420
<b>Because think about it,</b>

4452
02:16:05,420 --> 02:16:06,621
<b>research as a</b>

4453
02:16:06,621 --> 02:16:07,455
<b>career,</b>

4454
02:16:07,455 --> 02:16:08,756
<b>a researcher as a</b>

4455
